<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Taizong of Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/626">626</a>–<a href="/years/649">649</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Li Shimin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Taizong, born Li Shimin, was the second emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> (r. 626–649). He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a>, expanded Tang power into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and consolidated the institutions of government. He is remembered as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s greatest rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Taizong of Tang" width="203" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TangTaizong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Shimin was born in 598 at Wugong, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. His father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a>, Duke of Tang, was a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> and a nephew by marriage of the Sui founder, Emperor Wen, and the family was partly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> descent. In 613 he married the niece of the official Gao Shilian, the later Empress Zhangsun. He is traditionally regarded as a co-founder of the Tang because in 617 he encouraged his father to rebel against the Sui at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinyang">Jinyang</a>.</p><p>Under his father, Emperor Gaozu, he led campaigns to reunify the empire. Against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-ju">Xue Ju</a> he suffered what was recorded as his only defeat until the Goguryeo campaign of 645, when his assistants ignored his orders not to engage. His successes led to an intense rivalry with his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jiancheng">Li Jiancheng</a>, the crown prince, and the court divided into two factions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Taizong took the throne in 626 and made his wife empress and their eldest son, Li Chengqian, crown prince. Within 19 days the Eastern Turkic leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illig-qaghan">Illig Qaghan</a> reached the Wei river across from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and Taizong had to negotiate with him in person, promising tribute. In 630 his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jing">Li Jing</a> defeated and captured the khan, destroying Eastern Turkic power, and Taizong took the title Khan of Heaven. His armies annexed Karakhoja in 640, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Karasahr</a> in 644 and Kucha in 648.</p><p>He employed capable chancellors such as Fang Xuanling, Du Ruhui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-zheng">Wei Zheng</a> and tolerated criticism, and his era, Zhenguan (627 to 649), is regarded in tradition as a golden age and a model for later emperors. He also allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Syriac Christianity to exist in the empire. After 642 officials split over the rivalry of Li Chengqian and his brother Li Tai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Taizong fell seriously ill in the summer of 649, entrusted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-tang">Li Zhi</a> to Zhangsun Wuji and Chu Suiliang, and died at the Cuiwei Palace. His death was kept secret for three days, and Li Zhi took the throne as Emperor Gaozong. Six stone reliefs of his favourite horses were carved for his tomb between 636 and 649; four are now in the Beilin Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a> and two in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Taizong of Tang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goguryeo's defences met their greatest test in the wars with Sui and Tang: Sui Wendi's expedition of 598 failed, Sui Yangdi's huge army of 612 was routed by Eulji Mundeok at the Salsu River, and in 645 <b>Tang Taizong</b> took Liaodong fortress but abandoned the siege of Ansi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the dynasty's last years rebellion drew able-bodied men away from the farms; some deliberately broke their limbs to avoid conscription, calling it 'propitious paws' and 'fortunate feet', a practice <b>Emperor Taizong of Tang</b> was still trying to stamp out in 642.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Li Yuan's second son, <b>Li Shimin</b>, was the decisive commander in the campaigns that secured the dynasty, and in 626 staged the Xuanwu Gate Incident, an ambush in which he killed his brothers, the crown prince Li Jiancheng and Li Yuanji, before compelling his father to abdicate in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 634 he sent his first embassy to Tang China, and after a Tibetan attack on the Tang frontier at Songzhou in 638 the emperor <b>Taizong</b> agreed to a marriage alliance: Princess Wencheng arrived in Tibet in 641.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The monk Xuanzang (602–664), of Jingtu Temple in the capital Chang'an, left in 629, in defiance of <b>Emperor Taizong of Tang</b>'s ban on travel, to seek Buddhist scriptures in India.</blockquote><a href="/work/journey-to-the-west">Journey to the West · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Li Shimin</b> ambushed and killed his brothers Li Jiancheng and Li Yuanji at the Xuanwu Gate of Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wugong County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Hangfeng Hall. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.26,108.21&amp;year=637" aria-label="Wugong County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7303 922 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Taizong of Tang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7753 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7753 1192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.26,108.21&amp;year=637">What was here in 637 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/emperor-gaozu-of-tang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Emperor Gaozu of Tang</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; founded dynasty 618 CE</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-tang"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Emperor Gaozong of Tang</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 628–683</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You may use copper as a mirror for the person; you may use the past as a mirror for politics; and you may use man as a mirror to guide one’s judgment in ordinary affairs.&#8221;</p><footer>On the death of his minister Wei Zheng, 643 (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
